1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,887
Chapter 47: Fetal-Growth Disorders.

3
00:00:05,887 --> 00:00:19,950
Normal Fetal Growth Fetal-Growth Restriction Detection Risk Factors Management Fetal Macrosomia Risk Factors Management References Disorders of fetal growth occur at both ends of the spectrum, either fetalgrowth restriction or macrosomia.

4
00:00:19,950 --> 00:00:25,350
Each poses concern because of associated morbidities and potential mortality.

5
00:00:25,350 --> 00:00:33,912
However, in both categories most of these newborns are ultimately deemed normal and healthy but merely constitutionally small or large.

6
00:00:33,912 --> 00:00:40,325
The clinical challenge thus lies in the evaluation and management of suspected fetal-growth disorders.

7
00:00:40,325 --> 00:00:42,475
Normal Fetal Growth.

8
00:00:42,475 --> 00:00:46,000
Fetal growth may be divided into three phases.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:53,612
The initial phase of hyperplasia occurs in the first 16 weeks and is characterized by a rapid rise in cell number.

10
00:00:53,612 --> 00:01:01,637
The second phase, which extends up to 32 weeks’ gestation, includes both cellular hyperplasia and hypertrophy.

11
00:01:01,637 --> 00:01:10,950
After 32 weeks, fetal mass accrues by cellular hypertrophy, and it is during this phase that most fetal fat and glycogen accumulate.

12
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:28,200
The corresponding fetal-growth rates during these three phases approximate 5 g/d at 15 weeks’ gestation, 15 g/d at 24 weeks’, and 30 g/d at 34 weeks’ (Grantz, 2018; Williams, 1982).

13
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:42,225
In the National Institute of Child Health and Human Development Fetal Growth Studies, serial sonographic evaluations were performed in 1733 nonobese, low-risk pregnancies at 12 sites across the United States .

14
00:01:42,225 --> 00:01:48,662
As shown in Figure 47-1, growth velocity peaked at 35 weeks’ gestation .

15
00:01:48,662 --> 00:01:56,875
The investigators demonstrated that fetal growth varies considerably and that it is not highly correlated with fetal birthweight percentile.

16
00:01:56,875 --> 00:02:09,762
Some fetuses with initial estimated weights below the 5th percentile maintained their growth velocity and ultimately weighed more at birth than other fetuses whose weight percentiles were initially higher but whose growth was slower.

17
00:02:09,762 --> 00:02:19,600
Such findings support our understanding that large and small fetal weight percentiles reflect constitutional size in some but indicate disordered growth in others.

18
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:25,275
They further highlight the importance of serial sonography when abnormal growth is a concern.

19
00:02:25,275 --> 00:02:27,900
Figure 47-1.

20
00:02:27,900 --> 00:02:33,212
Fetal weight gain in grams per day by race/ethnicity throughout gestation.

21
00:02:33,212 --> 00:02:35,712
# Normal Birthweight.

22
00:02:35,712 --> 00:02:41,350
Accurate gestational age assessment is critical for determining whether birthweight is normal.

23
00:02:41,350 --> 00:02:54,162
Current normative data are based on birthweights from pregnancies in which gestational age is established using an obstetrical estimate that includes sonography and discussed in Chapter 14 (p. 248) .

24
00:02:54,162 --> 00:03:05,262
The birthweight percentiles shown in Table 47-1 were derived using data from more than 3 million liveborn singletons delivered across the United States in 2011 .

25
00:03:05,262 --> 00:03:20,312
As shown in Figure 47-2, use of a birthweight percentile curve in which gestational age is based on a last menstrual period alone yields significantly larger weights for a given gestational age, particularly in the preterm period.

26
00:03:20,312 --> 00:03:29,000
The accuracy of a birthweight reference thus has potential to affect the prevalence of neonates diagnosed as small or large for gestational age.

27
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,562
Table 47-1.

28
00:03:31,562 --> 00:03:43,425
2011 Gestational Age Birthweight (g) Percentiles for 3,252,011 Singleton Live Births in the United States Figure 47-2.

29
00:03:43,425 --> 00:03:48,212
Fetal-growth curves for births in the United States in 2011.

30
00:03:48,212 --> 00:03:58,662
Curves vary depending on whether gestational age was calculated from the last menstrual period or from an improved obstetrical estimate, derived in part using sonography.

31
00:03:58,662 --> 00:04:08,450
Modified with permission from Duryea EL, Hawkins JS, McIntire DD, et al: A revised birth weight reference for the United States.

32
00:04:08,450 --> 00:04:15,062
Obstet Gynecol. 2014 Jul;124(1):16–22.

33
00:04:15,062 --> 00:04:23,637
The curve by Duryea and associates (2014) is most accurately termed a population reference, rather than a standard.

34
00:04:23,637 --> 00:04:31,937
A population reference incorporates pregnancies of varying risks, along with the resulting outcomes, both normal and abnormal.

35
00:04:31,937 --> 00:04:37,475
In contrast, a standard incorporates normal pregnancies with normal outcomes.

36
00:04:37,475 --> 00:04:46,175
Because population references include preterm births, which are more likely to be growth restricted, it has been argued that the associated

37
00:04:46,324 --> 00:04:53,799
birthweight data overestimate impaired fetal growth (Mayer, 2013; Zhang, 2010).

38
00:04:53,749 --> 00:04:56,936
# Physiology of Fetal Growth.

39
00:04:56,936 --> 00:05:06,649
Fetal development is believed to be determined by maternal provision of substrate and its placental transfer, whereas fetal growth potential is governed by the genome.

40
00:05:06,649 --> 00:05:13,211
The precise cellular and molecular mechanisms leading to normal fetal growth are incompletely understood.

41
00:05:13,211 --> 00:05:20,949
Considerable evidence supports the role of insulin and insulin-like growth factors in fetal growth and weight gain regulation .

42
00:05:20,949 --> 00:05:28,486
These growth factors are produced by virtually all organs and are potent stimulators of cell division and differentiation.

43
00:05:28,486 --> 00:05:36,161
Other hormones implicated in fetal growth include leptin and other adipokines, which are derived from adipose tissue.

44
00:05:36,161 --> 00:05:49,999
Fetal leptin concentrations rise during gestation, and they correlate both with birthweight and with neonatal fat mass (Briffa, 2015; Ökdemir, 2018; Simpson, 2017).

45
00:05:49,999 --> 00:05:56,824
Both excessive and diminished maternal glucose availability also have the potential to affect fetal growth .

46
00:05:56,824 --> 00:06:04,549
That said, growthrestricted neonates do not typically show pathologically low glucose concentrations in cord blood .

47
00:06:04,549 --> 00:06:13,124
Fetal-growth restriction in response to glucose deprivation generally results only after long-term severe maternal caloric deprivation .

48
00:06:13,124 --> 00:06:18,499
Conversely, hyperglycemia more consistently results in excessive growth.

49
00:06:18,499 --> 00:06:33,486
The Hyperglycemia and Adverse Pregnancy Outcomes (HAPO) Study Cooperative Research Group (2008) found that elevated cord C-peptide levels, which reflect fetal hyperinsulinemia, are associated with greater birthweight.

50
00:06:33,486 --> 00:06:40,099
This relationship was noted even in women with maternal glucose levels below the threshold for diabetes.

51
00:06:40,099 --> 00:06:45,374
Excessive transfer of lipids has similarly been implicated in fetal overgrowth .

52
00:06:45,374 --> 00:07:00,111
Free or nonesterified fatty acids in maternal plasma may be transferred to the fetus via facilitated diffusion or after liberation of fatty acids from triglycerides by trophoblastic lipases (GilSánchez, 2012).

53
00:07:00,111 --> 00:07:10,149
Independent of prepregnancy body mass index (BMI), higher maternal free fatty acid levels during the latter half of pregnancy correlate with birthweight .

54
00:07:10,149 --> 00:07:17,174
Greater intake of certain fatty acids, particularly omega-3, is associated with greater birthweight .

55
00:07:17,174 --> 00:07:26,724
Placental fatty acid metabolism and transfer may be dysregulated in fetalgrowth restriction and in maternal conditions associated with fetal overgrowth.

56
00:07:26,724 --> 00:07:39,961
For example, levels of endothelial lipase are reduced with deficient fetal growth, and this enzyme is overexpressed in placentas of women with diabetes (Gauster, 2007, 2011).

57
00:07:39,961 --> 00:07:47,236
Others have reported that diabetes and obesity are associated with altered placental lipid-transport gene expression .

58
00:07:47,236 --> 00:07:54,811
Obesity is also linked with greater expression of fatty acid binding/transport proteins within the trophoblast .

59
00:07:54,811 --> 00:08:09,586
These alterations lead to an abnormal accumulation of lipids that can result in pathological placental inflammation and dysfunction (Calabuig-Navarro, 2016; Myatt, 2016; Yang, 2016).

60
00:08:09,586 --> 00:08:11,849
Fetal-Growth Restriction.

61
00:08:11,849 --> 00:08:20,961
Fundamental to understanding fetal-growth restriction (FGR) is an appreciation of the strengths and limitations of its defining criteria.

62
00:08:20,961 --> 00:08:29,136
A diagnosis based on estimated fetal weight alone does not indicate disease but rather a fetus that is in an at-risk category.

63
00:08:29,136 --> 00:08:40,111
Benefits of definitions such as the one used by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists are ease of application and promotion of consistency across care settings.

64
00:08:40,111 --> 00:08:53,524
However, if applied to an otherwise low-risk population, a 10th-percentile threshold will label nearly 10 percent of fetuses as growth restricted, although most are merely constitutionally small rather than compromised.

65
00:08:53,524 --> 00:09:01,861
Moreover, a threshold will fail to identify at-risk fetuses with a weight above that threshold but who have not reached their growth potential.

66
00:09:01,861 --> 00:09:08,836
Thus, the definition is intended to strike a balance between false-positive and falsenegative diagnoses.

67
00:09:08,836 --> 00:09:11,061
# Definition.

68
00:09:11,061 --> 00:09:15,649
Various criteria and thresholds have been used to define FGR.

69
00:09:15,649 --> 00:09:23,986
These have included estimated fetal weights (EFW) below the 3rd, 5th, or 10th percentiles; similar abdominal

70
00:09:24,148 --> 00:09:35,798
circumference (AC) percentiles; a specified decline in the EFW percentile or AC percentile over serial assessments; and various abnormal Doppler findings .

71
00:09:35,748 --> 00:09:42,648
All definitions are based solely on prenatal sonography and rely on accurate gestational age assessment.

72
00:09:42,648 --> 00:10:01,185
Currently, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021a) and the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2020) recommend defining FGR as either an EFW <10th percentile for gestational age or an AC <10th percentile for gestational age.

73
00:10:01,185 --> 00:10:12,498
Use of the AC threshold may correctly identify one additional small-for-gestational age (SGA) newborn for every 14 sonograms performed in the last month of pregnancy .

74
00:10:12,498 --> 00:10:27,635
Importantly, management recommendations are further stratified based on lower percentile thresholds and findings such as oligohydramnios or abnormal umbilical artery Doppler velocimetry, as discussed subsequently (p. 829).

75
00:10:27,635 --> 00:10:41,398
To more precisely identify growth-restricted fetuses, investigators have derived customized growth charts that incorporate variables such as maternal height and weight, race or ethnicity, parity, and fetal sex.

76
00:10:41,398 --> 00:11:00,035
However, customized growth curves do not improve outcomes and thus are not recommended (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2021a; Chiossi, 2017; Costantine, 2013; Grobman, 2013; Zhang, 2011).

77
00:11:00,035 --> 00:11:07,248
FGR should be differentiated from SGA, which is a postnatal designation based on birthweight percentile.

78
00:11:07,248 --> 00:11:16,623
When considering FGR detection, investigators study the proportion of SGA neonates identified as FGR during prenatal sonography.

79
00:11:16,623 --> 00:11:23,648
However, FGR often does not equate to SGA because of the inherent error of sonographic measurement.

80
00:11:23,648 --> 00:11:33,623
One population-based study of routine third-trimester sonography found that 50 percent of those with suspected FGR had birthweights above the 10th percentile .

81
00:11:33,623 --> 00:11:44,498
Those with false-positive diagnoses of FGR were delivered 2 weeks earlier in gestation, which raises concerns about potential iatrogenic sequelae of routine screening.

82
00:11:44,498 --> 00:11:51,210
Importantly, as many as 70 percent of SGA newborns are not pathologically growth restricted.

83
00:11:51,210 --> 00:12:00,523
Indeed, such children have normal outcomes and are thought to be appropriately grown when maternal ethnic group, parity, weight, and height are considered .

84
00:12:00,523 --> 00:12:11,573
In a Swedish study of 130,000 term births, the maternal and paternal birthweights were estimated to account for 6 and 3 percent of variance in birthweight, respectively .

85
00:12:11,573 --> 00:12:19,760
Additionally, as shown in Figure 47-3, most adverse outcomes occur in newborns smaller than the 3rd percentile .

86
00:12:19,760 --> 00:12:35,673
In a review of more than 80,000 singleton term births from Parkland Hospital, McIntire and colleagues (1999) found that neonatal mortality and most neonatal morbidity rates rose only at birthweights at or below the 3rd percentile.

87
00:12:35,673 --> 00:12:38,335
Figure 47-3.

88
00:12:38,335 --> 00:12:47,160
Relationship between birthweight percentile and perinatal mortality and morbidity rates in 1560 small-for-gestational-age fetuses.

89
00:12:47,160 --> 00:12:54,023
A progressive increase in both mortality and morbidity rates is observed as birthweight percentile decreases.

90
00:12:54,023 --> 00:13:02,335
Small-but-normal neonates do not show evidence of the postnatal metabolic derangements commonly associated with deficient fetal growth.

91
00:13:02,335 --> 00:13:12,610
Moreover, these intrinsically small newborns remain significantly smaller during surveillance to 2 years compared with appropriate-for-gestational-age sized neonates.

92
00:13:12,610 --> 00:13:19,648
However, they do not show differences in measures of metabolic risk that included glucose and insulin levels .

93
00:13:19,648 --> 00:13:25,748
As discussed later, true SGA newborns carry short- and long-term metabolic risks.

94
00:13:25,748 --> 00:13:27,923
# Detection.

95
00:13:27,923 --> 00:13:32,573
Identification of truly impaired fetal growth remains a challenge.

96
00:13:32,573 --> 00:13:40,535
Ideally, it involves detecting fetuses who meet the aforementioned definition and also discerning those at risk for compromise.

97
00:13:40,535 --> 00:13:54,785
There are three main tenets: 1. The diagnosis relies on accurate gestational age assessment, optimally confirmed with sonography in the first or early second trimester (Table 141, p. 248).

98
00:13:54,785 --> 00:14:01,548
If gestational age is uncertain and the diagnosis is suspected, serial sonography is considered.

99
00:14:01,548 --> 00:14:08,485
2. In low-risk pregnancies, the diagnosis is suspected based on clinical abdominal examination

100
00:14:08,644 --> 00:14:17,356
after 24 weeks’ gestation in which the fundal height lags by ≥3 cm. Sonography is then performed.

101
00:14:17,306 --> 00:14:26,606
3. If the pregnancy is at risk for FGR, based on factors reviewed in the next section, sonography is considered to assist with detection.

102
00:14:26,606 --> 00:14:30,894
This is performed at approximately 32 weeks’ gestation.

103
00:14:30,894 --> 00:14:38,319
Care is individualized, and in some cases sonography may be needed every 4 weeks to assess fetal growth.

104
00:14:38,319 --> 00:14:45,606
One example of the latter is twin gestations, because fundal height cannot evaluate individual twin growth .

105
00:14:45,606 --> 00:14:54,069
Uterine Fundal Height At each prenatal visit after 20 weeks’ gestation, fundal height is assessed to screen for fetal-growth impairment .

106
00:14:54,069 --> 00:14:58,994
The measurement in centimeters approximates the gestational age in weeks.

107
00:14:58,994 --> 00:15:03,881
The uterine size-date discrepancy may be unrelated to fetal size.

108
00:15:03,881 --> 00:15:13,869
However, if the measurement differs by ≥3 cm in the absence of an obvious explanation, sonography is generally performed.

109
00:15:13,869 --> 00:15:26,756
Serial fundal height measurement is the only routine screening for FGR that is endorsed by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2021a).

110
00:15:26,756 --> 00:15:38,494
International consensus also indicates that carefully performed serial fundal height measurements are a simple, safe, inexpensive, and reasonably accurate method to screen for FGR .

111
00:15:38,494 --> 00:15:42,619
Limitations of fundal height screening are well documented.

112
00:15:42,619 --> 00:15:56,831
The overall sensitivity of fundal height to detect FGR ranges from 11 to 25 percent (Goetzinger, 2012; Grantz, 2021; Roex, 2012; Sparks, 2011).

113
00:15:56,831 --> 00:16:01,719
Detection rates are lower in obese women than in those with normal BMI .

114
00:16:01,719 --> 00:16:23,831
Sonographic Assessment Studies of routine screening for FGR using third-trimester sonography have yielded variable results, and detection of SGA neonates ranges from <20 percent to >60 percent (Hammad, 2016; Monier, 2015; Sovio, 2015; Wanyonyi, 2021).

115
00:16:23,831 --> 00:16:32,869
One reason for poor detection is that sonography performed at any point in gestation will fail to detect pregnancies in which FGR develops later.

116
00:16:32,869 --> 00:16:39,644
Also, it will miss those needing delivery for FGR before the sonographic examination was performed.

117
00:16:39,644 --> 00:16:43,994
Compounding this is that EFW is an imperfect metric.

118
00:16:43,994 --> 00:16:53,219
Given that most SGA neonates are constitutionally small, it is not unexpected that the recurrence risk for FGR approximates 20 percent.

119
00:16:53,219 --> 00:16:59,806
Many of these cases are merely smaller mothers having smaller healthy children in successive pregnancies.

120
00:16:59,806 --> 00:17:07,969
Routine third-trimester sonography to assess fetal growth is not recommended because it has not been demonstrated to improve outcomes .

121
00:17:07,969 --> 00:17:22,669
Indeed, a Cochrane database analysis of 13 trials with 34,980 women found that routine late-pregnancy ultrasound for a low-risk or an unselected population was not associated with maternal or fetal benefit .

122
00:17:22,669 --> 00:17:25,469
# Pathophysiology.

123
00:17:25,469 --> 00:17:32,631
Fetal-growth restriction is one of the “major obstetrical syndromes” associated with defects in early placentation .

124
00:17:32,631 --> 00:17:42,531
Mechanisms leading to abnormal trophoblastic invasion are likely multifactorial, and both vascular and immunological etiologies have been proposed.

125
00:17:42,531 --> 00:17:54,219
For example, atrial natriuretic peptide converting enzyme, also known as corin, plays a critical role in trophoblastic invasion and remodeling of the uterine spiral arteries .

126
00:17:54,219 --> 00:18:00,781
These processes are impaired in corin-deficient mice, which also develop evidence of preeclampsia.

127
00:18:00,781 --> 00:18:06,544
Moreover, mutations in the gene for corin are reported in women with preeclampsia .

128
00:18:06,544 --> 00:18:11,531
Several immunological abnormalities are associated with FGR.

129
00:18:11,531 --> 00:18:16,769
This raises the prospect of maternal rejection of the “paternal semiallograft.

130
00:18:16,769 --> 00:18:27,144
Rudzinski and colleagues (2013) studied C4d, a component of complement that is associated with humoral rejection of transplanted tissues.

131
00:18:27,144 --> 00:18:36,969
They found this to be highly associated with chronic villitis—88 percent of cases versus only 5 percent of controls—and with reduced placental weight.

132
00:18:36,969 --> 00:18:48,006
In a study of 10,204 placentas, chronic villitis was associated with placental hypoperfusion, fetal acidemia, and FGR and its sequelae .

133
00:18:48,006 --> 00:18:59,106
Kim and coworkers (2015) extensively reviewed chronic inflammatory placental lesions and their association with fetal-growth restriction, preeclampsia, and

134
00:18:59,260 --> 00:19:01,160
preterm birth.

135
00:19:01,110 --> 00:19:03,497
# Risk Factors.

136
00:19:03,497 --> 00:19:13,185
Risk factors for impaired fetal growth may be divided into three overlapping categories or “compartments”: those in the mother, the fetus, or the placenta.

137
00:19:13,185 --> 00:19:17,172
Some of these are depicted in Figure 47-4.

138
00:19:17,172 --> 00:19:25,972
Many causes of FGR are prospectively considered risk factors, because impaired fetal growth is not consistent in all affected women.

139
00:19:25,972 --> 00:19:28,685
Figure 47-4.

140
00:19:28,685 --> 00:19:34,960
Risk factors and causes of impaired fetal growth centering on the mother, fetus, and placenta.

141
00:19:34,960 --> 00:19:42,060
Gestational Weight Gain and Nutrition Maternal weight gain during pregnancy is positively correlated with fetal size .

142
00:19:42,060 --> 00:19:50,547
Among women with gestational diabetes, gestational weight gain is also associated with both neonatal birthweight and adiposity .

143
00:19:50,547 --> 00:20:05,885
In contrast, a gestational weight gain during the second and third trimesters that is less than that recommended by the Institute of Medicine is associated with increased risk of an SGA neonate in women of all weight categories except class II or III obesity .

144
00:20:05,885 --> 00:20:12,210
The best-documented effect of famine on fetal growth was in the winter of 1944 in Holland.

145
00:20:12,210 --> 00:20:22,222
For 6 months, the German occupation army restricted dietary intake to 500 kcal/d for civilians, including pregnant women.

146
00:20:22,222 --> 00:20:27,222
As a result, the average birthweight declined by 250 g .

147
00:20:27,222 --> 00:20:31,860
Undernourished women may benefit from micronutrient supplementation.

148
00:20:31,860 --> 00:20:41,810
In one study, almost 32,000 Indonesian women were randomly assigned to receive micronutrient supplementation or only iron and folate tablets .

149
00:20:41,810 --> 00:20:51,260
Offspring of those receiving the supplement had lower risks of early infant mortality and low birthweight and had improved childhood motor and cognitive abilities.

150
00:20:51,260 --> 00:21:02,235
A Cochrane review of 20 trials involving 141,849 women concluded that supplementation of micronutrients may lower the risk of low birthweight .

151
00:21:02,235 --> 00:21:09,097
The importance of antenatal vitamins and trace metals is discussed in Chapter 10 (p. 185).

152
00:21:09,097 --> 00:21:20,385
Socioeconomic Factors The effect of social deprivation on birthweight is interconnected with lifestyle factors such as smoking, alcohol or other substance abuse, and poor nutrition.

153
00:21:20,385 --> 00:21:32,897
With appropriate modifying interventions, women with psychosocial factors were significantly less likely to deliver a lowbirthweight newborn and also had fewer preterm births and other pregnancy complications .

154
00:21:32,897 --> 00:21:41,597
Food insecurity, late entry into prenatal care, and limited access to healthcare are all contributors to fetal-growth restriction .

155
00:21:41,597 --> 00:21:52,072
Hall (2020) found that in a military population with equal access to healthcare, racial differences in late entry to prenatal care and FGR persist.

156
00:21:52,072 --> 00:22:00,810
An Australian study showed that the proportion of pregnancies affected by fetal-growth restriction increased as levels of social disadvantage rose .

157
00:22:00,810 --> 00:22:10,210
Vascular and Renal Disease Especially when complicated by superimposed preeclampsia, chronic vascular disease commonly restricts fetal growth .

158
00:22:10,210 --> 00:22:27,272
Maternal vascular disease as evidenced by abnormal uterine artery Doppler velocimetry early in pregnancy is associated with higher rates of preeclampsia, SGA neonates, and delivery before 34 weeks (He, 2020; Poon, 2019).

159
00:22:27,272 --> 00:22:39,510
Using Washington state birth certificate data, Leary and colleagues (2012) found that maternal ischemic heart disease was associated with a 16-percent risk of having an SGA newborn.

160
00:22:39,510 --> 00:22:46,335
Chronic renal insufficiency is frequently associated with underlying hypertension and vascular disease.

161
00:22:46,335 --> 00:22:56,885
Nephropathies are commonly accompanied by restricted fetal growth (Cunningham, 1990; Feng, 2015; Saliem, 2016).

162
00:22:56,885 --> 00:23:03,010
These relationships are considered further in Chapter 56 (p. 1004).

163
00:23:03,010 --> 00:23:15,422
Pregestational Diabetes Fetal-growth restriction in newborns of women with diabetes may be related to congenital malformations or may follow substrate deprivation from advanced maternal vascular disease .

164
00:23:15,422 --> 00:23:22,372
The likelihood of restricted growth increases with worsening White classification, particularly nephropathy .

165
00:23:22,372 --> 00:23:29,235
That said, the prevalence of serious vascular disease associated with diabetes in pregnancy is low.

166
00:23:29,235 --> 00:23:40,097
Chronic Hypoxia Conditions associated with chronic hypoxia include asthma, maternal cyanotic heart disease, other chronic pulmonary disease, cigarette smoking,

167
00:23:40,252 --> 00:23:42,752
and living at high altitude.

168
00:23:42,702 --> 00:23:49,452
When exposed to a chronically hypoxic environment, some fetuses have significantly reduced birthweight.

169
00:23:49,452 --> 00:24:00,727
Smoking causes a dose-dependent reduction in fetal growth, resulting in an average birthweight 200 g below that of newborns of nonsmokers (D’Souza, 1981).

170
00:24:00,727 --> 00:24:10,327
For each 1000-meter rise in altitude, the birthweight declined 150 g in a study of more than 1.8 million births in Austria .

171
00:24:10,327 --> 00:24:15,614
Anemia In most cases, maternal anemia does not impair fetal growth.

172
00:24:15,614 --> 00:24:24,539
Exceptions include sickle-cell disease and other inherited anemias (Desai, 2017; Thame, 2016).

173
00:24:24,539 --> 00:24:34,064
Importantly, curtailed maternal blood-volume expansion is linked to FGR (de Haas, 2017; Stott, 2017).

174
00:24:34,064 --> 00:24:38,452
This is further discussed in Chapter 4 (p. 59).

175
00:24:38,452 --> 00:24:55,377
Antiphospholipid Syndrome Adverse obstetrical outcomes including fetal-growth restriction have been associated with three types of antiphospholipid antibodies: anticardiolipin antibodies, lupus anticoagulant, and anti-β2 glycoprotein-I antibodies.

176
00:24:55,377 --> 00:25:04,189
Mechanistically, a “two-hit” hypothesis suggests that initial endothelial damage is then followed by intervillous placental thrombosis.

177
00:25:04,189 --> 00:25:15,889
More specifically, oxidative damage to certain membrane proteins is followed by antiphospholipid antibody binding, which leads to immune-complex formation and ultimately to thrombosis .

178
00:25:15,889 --> 00:25:24,564
This syndrome is considered in detail in Chapters 55 (p. 979) and 62 (p. 1115).

179
00:25:24,564 --> 00:25:32,839
Women with more than one type of antiphospholipid antibody are at higher risk for FGR compared with those with only one antibody .

180
00:25:32,839 --> 00:25:42,027
Also, anti-β2 glycoprotein-I antibodies may have a stronger association with FGR, particularly early-onset disease.

181
00:25:42,027 --> 00:25:53,564
Placental, Cord, and Uterine Abnormalities Several placental abnormalities are associated with poor fetal growth, which is presumed secondary to uteroplacental insufficiency.

182
00:25:53,564 --> 00:26:05,789
These are discussed further throughout Chapter 6 and include chronic placental abruption, extensive infarction, chorioangioma, velamentous cord insertion, and umbilical artery thrombosis.

183
00:26:05,789 --> 00:26:12,177
Abnormal placental implantation leading to endothelial dysfunction may also limit fetal growth .

184
00:26:12,177 --> 00:26:17,427
This pathology is implicated in pregnancies complicated by preeclampsia .

185
00:26:17,427 --> 00:26:22,389
Last, some uterine malformations are linked to impaired fetal growth .

186
00:26:22,389 --> 00:26:32,364
Multifetal Gestation Pregnancy with two or more fetuses is more likely to be complicated by diminished growth of one or more fetuses compared with that of singletons .

187
00:26:32,364 --> 00:26:36,102
Serial sonography is recommended for this reason.

188
00:26:36,102 --> 00:26:46,589
With the understanding that normal twin growth may be less than that of singletons, we use a chorionicity-specific twin nomogram to diagnose FGR at Parkland Hospital.

189
00:26:46,589 --> 00:26:52,264
Discordance in estimated fetal weight also is considered when evaluating twin growth .

190
00:26:52,264 --> 00:26:55,064
Figure 47-5.

191
00:26:55,064 --> 00:27:02,914
Birthweight and gestational age relationships in multifetal gestations without malformations delivered at Parkland Hospital.

192
00:27:02,914 --> 00:27:09,252
Medications and Other Substances Fortunately, most medications do not affect fetal growth.

193
00:27:09,252 --> 00:27:17,489
Selected medications and other substances that have been associated with FGR due to fetotoxicity are discussed in Chapter 8.

194
00:27:17,489 --> 00:27:26,977
Examples include cyclophosphamide and other antineoplastic drugs, significant lead exposure, and drugs such as cocaine and methamphetamine.

195
00:27:26,977 --> 00:27:31,339
Alcohol and tobacco each have potent effects on fetal growth.

196
00:27:31,339 --> 00:27:42,102
FGR is included among the diagnostic criteria for fetal alcohol syndrome, and cigarette smoking is associated with a two- to threefold increased risk of FGR .

197
00:27:42,102 --> 00:27:55,089
Maternal and Fetal Infections Viral, bacterial, protozoan, and spirochetal infections have been implicated in up to 5 percent of FGR cases and are discussed in Chapters 67 and 68.

198
00:27:55,089 --> 00:27:59,664
The best known of these are rubella and cytomegalovirus infection.

199
00:27:59,664 --> 00:28:08,527
Both promote calcifications in the fetus that are associated with cell death, and infection earlier in pregnancy correlates with worse outcomes.

200
00:28:08,527 --> 00:28:19,952
Toda and colleagues (2015) described a Vietnamese epidemic in which 39 percent of 292 term newborns with congenital rubella syndrome were low birthweight.

201
00:28:19,952 --> 00:28:29,139
In one study of 238 primary cytomegalovirus infections, no severe cases were observed when infection occurred after 14

202
00:28:29,308 --> 00:28:31,345
weeks’ gestation .

203
00:28:31,295 --> 00:28:43,008
These investigators later identified sonographic findings in 30 of 69 cases of congenital infection, and growth restriction was noted in 30 percent of these 30 cases .

204
00:28:43,008 --> 00:28:48,033
Tuberculosis and syphilis also are associated with poor fetal growth.

205
00:28:48,033 --> 00:28:53,470
Both extrapulmonary and pulmonary tuberculosis are linked with low birthweight .

206
00:28:53,470 --> 00:29:03,308
Sobhy (2017) analyzed 13 studies that included a total of 3384 women with active tuberculosis.

207
00:29:03,308 --> 00:29:07,333
The odds ratio was 1.7 for low birthweight.

208
00:29:07,333 --> 00:29:16,720
The etiology is uncertain, however, the adverse effects on maternal health, compounded by effects of poor nutrition and poverty, are important .

209
00:29:16,720 --> 00:29:27,133
Congenital syphilis is more common, and paradoxically, the placenta is almost always larger and heavier than normal due to edema and perivascular inflammation .

210
00:29:27,133 --> 00:29:32,958
Congenital syphilis is strongly linked with preterm birth and thus lowbirthweight newborns .

211
00:29:32,958 --> 00:29:38,283
Congenital infection with toxoplasma gondii is associated with FGR.

212
00:29:38,283 --> 00:29:46,795
Capobiango (2014) described 31 Brazilian pregnancies complicated by congenital toxoplasmosis.

213
00:29:46,795 --> 00:29:56,008
Only 13 percent were treated antepartum for toxoplasmosis, and low birthweight complicated nearly 40 percent of all the pregnancies.

214
00:29:56,008 --> 00:30:01,170
Congenital malaria may similarly cause low birthweight and poor fetal growth.

215
00:30:01,170 --> 00:30:09,070
Briand and colleagues (2016) emphasize the importance of prophylaxis early in pregnancy for women at risk.

216
00:30:09,070 --> 00:30:21,758
Congenital Malformations In a classic review of more than 13,000 fetuses with major malformations and chromosomal abnormalities, the rate of growth restriction was double the population prevalence .

217
00:30:21,758 --> 00:30:26,658
The birth defect most strongly linked with FGR is gastroschisis.

218
00:30:26,658 --> 00:30:35,645
Nelson (2015) reviewed 111 fetuses with gastroschisis and found that a third had birthweights <10th percentile.

219
00:30:35,645 --> 00:30:42,158
Congenital cardiac abnormalities are also associated with a slight increase in FGR risk.

220
00:30:42,158 --> 00:30:55,258
In a recent review of 1789 singleton neonates with isolated congenital cardiac abnormalities, the prevalence of SGA was 13 percent, which was 3 percent higher than the general-population risk .

221
00:30:55,258 --> 00:31:05,158
With few exceptions, the identification of growth restriction in the setting of a structural malformation further increases the risk for an underlying genetic syndrome.

222
00:31:05,158 --> 00:31:11,808
If not already performed, amniocentesis with chromosomal microarray analysis should be offered.

223
00:31:11,808 --> 00:31:19,770
Genetic Abnormalities Many genetic syndromes are strongly linked with prenatal-growth impairment or postnatal failure to thrive.

224
00:31:19,770 --> 00:31:32,258
Among liveborn neonates with autosomal trisomies, trisomy 21 is associated with an SGA prevalence of 15 to 30 percent (Herrera, 2020; Khoury, 1988).

225
00:31:32,258 --> 00:31:41,295
With trisomies 13 and 18, the risk of SGA is significantly greater, 50 percent and >80 percent, respectively .

226
00:31:41,295 --> 00:31:49,170
In trisomy 18, the combination of fetal abnormalities plus FGR and hydramnios is particularly common.

227
00:31:49,170 --> 00:32:02,808
The crown-rump length in fetuses with trisomy 18 and 13, unlike that with trisomy 21, is also typically shorter than expected (Bahado-Singh, 1997; Schemmer, 1997).

228
00:32:02,808 --> 00:32:12,370
Poor fetal growth similarly complicates Turner syndrome, and the severity correlates with increasing haploinsufficiency of the short arm of the X chromosome .

229
00:32:12,370 --> 00:32:22,358
In contrast, poor growth is not characteristic of an increased number of X chromosomes (Ottesen, 2010; Wigby, 2016).

230
00:32:22,358 --> 00:32:30,383
Discussed in Chapter 16 (p. 318), confined placental mosaicism is a recognized cause of FGR.

231
00:32:30,383 --> 00:32:32,608
# Management.

232
00:32:32,608 --> 00:32:39,258
If FGR is detected, efforts are made to assess the fetal condition and search for possible causes.

233
00:32:39,258 --> 00:32:45,708
The risk for stillbirth is increased, and early-onset growth restriction is especially problematic.

234
00:32:45,708 --> 00:32:57,233
General tenets of management include serial evaluation of fetal growth every 3 weeks and at least weekly evaluation of amnionic fluid and umbilical artery Doppler velocimetry.

235
00:32:57,233 --> 00:33:03,695
A fetus with slow but progressing EFW is more reassuring than one that has plateaued growth.

236
00:33:03,695 --> 00:33:12,208
This is supplemented with antepartum evaluation of fetal well-being, which is usually nonstress testing or biophysical profile .

237
00:33:12,208 --> 00:33:23,658
Consideration is also given to performing a detailed fetal anatomic survey and amniocentesis to assess for underlying genetic abnormalities or infection—particularly

238
00:33:23,812 --> 00:33:26,387
with early-onset FGR.

239
00:33:26,337 --> 00:33:30,987
A management algorithm is shown in Figure 47-6.

240
00:33:30,987 --> 00:33:33,824
Figure 47-6.

241
00:33:33,824 --> 00:33:37,587
Algorithm for management of fetal-growth restriction.

242
00:33:37,587 --> 00:33:53,699
EFW = estimated fetal weight; FLM = fetal lung maturity; NST = nonstress test; TORCH = toxoplasmosis, other, rubella, cytomegalovirus, herpesvirus; UA = umbilical artery.

243
00:33:53,699 --> 00:33:59,349
Delivery timing balances the risks of fetal death with the hazards of preterm birth.

244
00:33:59,349 --> 00:34:07,274
Several multicenter studies address these problems, but unfortunately, none has elucidated the optimal timing of delivery.

245
00:34:07,274 --> 00:34:15,237
For the preterm fetus, the only randomized trial of delivery timing is the Growth Restriction Intervention Trial (GRIT) .

246
00:34:15,237 --> 00:34:21,649
This trial involved 548 women between 24 and 36 weeks’ gestation.

247
00:34:21,649 --> 00:34:28,087
Women were randomly assigned to immediate delivery or to delayed delivery until the situation worsened.

248
00:34:28,087 --> 00:34:33,899
The primary outcome was perinatal death or disability after reaching age 2 years.

249
00:34:33,899 --> 00:34:37,824
Mortality rates did not differ through 2 years of age.

250
00:34:37,824 --> 00:34:45,399
Moreover, children aged 6 to 13 years did not show clinically significant differences between the two groups .

251
00:34:45,399 --> 00:34:57,112
The Disproportionate Intrauterine Growth Intervention Trial at Term (DIGITAT) study examined the delivery timing of growth-restricted fetuses who were 36 weeks’ gestation or older.

252
00:34:57,112 --> 00:35:03,887
The 321 enrolled women were randomly assigned to labor induction or to expectant management.

253
00:35:03,887 --> 00:35:16,424
Composite neonatal morbidity rates did not differ, except that neonatal admissions were lower after 38 weeks’ gestation in a secondary analysis (Boers, 2010, 2012).

254
00:35:16,424 --> 00:35:32,462
Secondary analyses of DIGITAT did not identify a clear subgroup that benefited from labor induction, and neurodevelopmental and behavioral outcomes at age 2 were similar in both groups (Tajik, 2014; Van Wyk, 2012).

255
00:35:32,462 --> 00:35:41,124
Doppler Velocimetry Umbilical artery Doppler flow studies are central to the evaluation and management of the fetus with growth restriction .

256
00:35:41,124 --> 00:35:46,649
Abnormalities represent the negative progression from fetal adaptation to failure.

257
00:35:46,649 --> 00:35:56,749
Specifically, initially increased impedance to flow in the umbilical artery may progress to absent end-diastolic flow and then reversed enddiastolic flow .

258
00:35:56,749 --> 00:36:02,574
This negative progression correlates with hypoxia, acidosis, and fetal death.

259
00:36:02,574 --> 00:36:19,649
In one prospective series of 1116 fetuses with EFW <10th percentile, only 1 percent of those with normal umbilical artery Doppler studies had adverse outcomes compared with 12 percent of fetuses with Doppler abnormalities (O’Dwyer, 2014).

260
00:36:19,649 --> 00:36:27,612
The stillbirth risk in the setting of absent and reversed end-diastolic flow is 7 percent and 19 percent, respectively .

261
00:36:27,612 --> 00:36:43,474
Because of these findings, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021a,b) and Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2020) recommend serial umbilical artery Doppler studies in the management of FGR.

262
00:36:43,474 --> 00:36:46,224
Figure 47-7.

263
00:36:46,224 --> 00:36:48,887
Doppler velocity waveforms.

264
00:36:48,887 --> 00:36:53,024
A. Normal waveform with normal S/D ratio.

265
00:36:53,024 --> 00:36:58,587
B. Increased impedance to flow with abnormally elevated S/D ratio.

266
00:36:58,587 --> 00:37:01,774
C. Absent end-diastolic flow.

267
00:37:01,774 --> 00:37:04,887
D. Reversed end-diastolic flow.

268
00:37:04,887 --> 00:37:25,312
Doppler abnormalities in other vessels may convey information regarding pathophysiology, but interrogation of these is not recommended for routine management of the pregnancy complicated by FGR (American college of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2021a; Society for MaternalFetal Medicine, 2020).

269
00:37:25,312 --> 00:37:39,237
Doppler abnormalities of the ductus venosus (Fig. 14-12, p. 264) reflect increased central venous pressure from decreased cardiac compliance and higher right ventricular end-diastolic pressure.

270
00:37:39,237 --> 00:37:49,737
Fetuses with abnormal ductus venosus Doppler flow have a 20-percent risk for stillbirth, and this increases to 46 percent in cases with a reversed Awave .

271
00:37:49,737 --> 00:37:55,787
Second, pulsatile flow in the umbilical vein waveform indicates cardiac dysfunction.

272
00:37:55,787 --> 00:38:03,362
Last, cerebral vasodilation is the fetal adaptative response to hypoxemia in the setting of growth restriction.

273
00:38:03,362 --> 00:38:18,399
The cerebroplacental ratio (CPR)—defined as middle cerebral artery pulsatility index divided by umbilical artery pulsatility index —is a measure of this adaptation and may be abnormal in severe cases of FGR.

274
00:38:18,399 --> 00:38:23,199
An abnormal CPR <1 has been associated with greater risk for

275
00:38:23,356 --> 00:38:35,356
earlier delivery, lower birthweight, cesarean delivery, neonatal intensive care unit admission, and perinatal death (DeVore, 2015; Flood, 2014).

276
00:38:35,306 --> 00:38:44,618
However, a metaanalysis of 18,731 fetuses found the CPR was not predictive of adverse perinatal outcomes .

277
00:38:44,618 --> 00:39:03,206
Management of the Near-term Fetus Delivery of a suspected growth-restricted fetus with normal umbilical artery Doppler velocimetry, normal amnionic fluid volume, and reassuring fetal testing can likely be deferred until 37 to 38 weeks’ gestation (see Fig. 47-6).

278
00:39:03,206 --> 00:39:10,381
Expectant management can be guided using antepartum evaluation of fetal well-being described in Chapter 20.

279
00:39:10,381 --> 00:39:27,481
If oligohydramnios is present, delivery between 360/7 and 376/7 weeks’ gestation is recommended (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2021c; Society for MaternalFetal Medicine, 2020).

280
00:39:27,481 --> 00:39:32,218
With a normal fetal heart rate pattern, vaginal delivery is planned.

281
00:39:32,218 --> 00:39:36,381
Notably, some of these fetuses do not tolerate labor.

282
00:39:36,381 --> 00:39:49,256
Management of the Fetus Remote from Term If growth restriction is identified in a fetus before 34 weeks, and amnionic fluid volume and fetal surveillance findings are normal, observation is recommended.

283
00:39:49,256 --> 00:39:58,906
As long as interval fetal growth and fetal surveillance test results are normal, pregnancy is allowed to continue (see Fig. 47-6).

284
00:39:58,906 --> 00:40:04,093
Reassessment of fetal growth is typically made no sooner than 3 weeks later.

285
00:40:04,093 --> 00:40:12,943
Weekly outpatient assessment of umbilical artery Doppler velocimetry and amnionic fluid volume is combined with fetal well-being testing.

286
00:40:12,943 --> 00:40:21,343
If umbilical artery Doppler studies indicate absent or reversed end-diastolic flow, inpatient surveillance is undertaken.

287
00:40:21,343 --> 00:40:32,081
During hospitalization, more frequent sonographic evaluations and antenatal testing of fetal well-being and close proximity to labor and delivery are advantages.

288
00:40:32,081 --> 00:40:37,943
With growth restriction remote from term, no specific treatment ameliorates the condition.

289
00:40:37,943 --> 00:40:44,556
Evidence does not support diminished activity or bed rest to accelerate growth or improve outcomes.

290
00:40:44,556 --> 00:40:54,943
Nutrient supplementation, attempts at plasma volume expansion, oxygen therapy, antihypertensive drugs, heparin, and aspirin are all ineffective .

291
00:40:54,943 --> 00:41:03,668
Management decisions hinge on assessment of the relative risks of fetal death during expectant management versus the risks from preterm delivery.

292
00:41:03,668 --> 00:41:10,168
Reassuring results from fetal well-being tests may allow observation with continued maturation.

293
00:41:10,168 --> 00:41:25,393
However, long-term neurological outcome, which theoretically may suffer from additional weeks in an inhospitable intrauterine environment, is a concern (Baschat, 2014; Lees, 2015; Thornton, 2004).

294
00:41:25,393 --> 00:41:36,156
Baschat and associates (2009) showed that neurodevelopmental outcome at 2 years in growth-restricted fetuses was best predicted by birthweight and gestational age.

295
00:41:36,156 --> 00:41:45,906
Doppler abnormalities are generally not associated with poor childhood cognitive developmental scores among low-birthweight fetuses delivered in the third trimester .

296
00:41:45,906 --> 00:41:52,031
These findings emphasize that adverse neurodevelopmental outcomes cannot always be predicted.

297
00:41:52,031 --> 00:41:54,818
# Intrapartum Management.

298
00:41:54,818 --> 00:42:05,731
When lagging fetal growth is the result of placental insufficiency due to poor maternal perfusion or reduction of functional placenta, the fetal condition may be aggravated by labor.

299
00:42:05,731 --> 00:42:11,931
Equally important, oligohydramnios raises the likelihood of cord compression during labor.

300
00:42:11,931 --> 00:42:17,218
For these and other reasons, the frequency of cesarean delivery is increased.

301
00:42:17,218 --> 00:42:22,568
The risk of neonatal hypoxia or meconium aspiration is also greater.

302
00:42:22,568 --> 00:42:31,106
Thus, care for the newborn should be provided immediately by an attendant who can skillfully clear the airway and ventilate a neonate as needed .

303
00:42:31,106 --> 00:42:43,281
The severely growth-restricted newborn is particularly susceptible to hypothermia and may also develop other metabolic derangements such as hypoglycemia, polycythemia, and hyperviscosity.

304
00:42:43,281 --> 00:42:52,393
Risk is greatest at the lowest extremes of birthweight (Baschat, 2009, 2014; Llurba, 2013).

305
00:42:52,393 --> 00:42:54,606
# Outcomes.

306
00:42:54,606 --> 00:42:59,506
Lessons learned from SGA neonates inform concerns about FGR.

307
00:42:59,506 --> 00:43:10,831
More than 50 years ago, Battaglia and Lubchenco (1967) classified SGA neonates as those whose weights were below the 10th percentile for their gestational age.

308
00:43:10,831 --> 00:43:12,156
The

309
00:43:12,316 --> 00:43:21,028
mortality rate of SGA neonates born at 38 weeks was 1 percent compared with 0.2 percent in those with larger birthweights.

310
00:43:20,978 --> 00:43:31,291
More recent data also indicate that the overall stillbirth rate among SGA neonates approximates 1 percent, which is twice as high as the population prevalence .

311
00:43:31,291 --> 00:43:36,978
The risk for abnormal neurological development also is greater in SGA neonates.

312
00:43:36,978 --> 00:43:54,753
In an analysis of nearly 3000 newborns born before 27 weeks’ gestation, those weighing <10th percentile had a nearly fourfold higher risk of neonatal death or neurodevelopmental impairment and a nearly threefold greater risk of cerebral palsy compared with non-SGA neonates .

313
00:43:54,753 --> 00:44:12,703
In another analysis of more than 91,000 otherwise uncomplicated pregnancies, newborns with birthweights <5th percentile had a higher risk of low 5-minute Apgar score, respiratory distress, necrotizing enterocolitis, and neonatal sepsis than appropriate-weight neonates.

314
00:44:12,703 --> 00:44:18,666
The risks of stillbirth and neonatal death were sixfold and fourfold higher, respectively .

315
00:44:18,666 --> 00:44:24,141
Newborns at the lowest birthweight percentiles are at greatest risk for adverse outcome.

316
00:44:24,141 --> 00:44:34,291
In one study of more than 44,561 neonates, only 14 percent of those weighing <1st percentile at birth survived to discharge .

317
00:44:34,291 --> 00:44:50,366
Poor motor, cognitive, language and attention, and behavioral outcomes in growth-restricted newborns unfortunately persist into early childhood and adolescence (Baschat, 2014; Levine, 2015; Rogne, 2015).

318
00:44:50,366 --> 00:45:00,353
Early-onset Growth Restriction Perinatal morbidities are further increased in the 30 percent of FGR pregnancies diagnosed prior to 32 weeks’ gestation .

319
00:45:00,353 --> 00:45:06,741
Thus, this gestational age is used to demarcate early-onset from late-onset growth restriction .

320
00:45:06,741 --> 00:45:20,828
Early-onset FGR is typically more severe and more commonly associated with placental dysfunction and maternal hypertension than late-onset growth restriction (Aviram, 2019; Dall’Asta, 2017).

321
00:45:20,828 --> 00:45:29,328
Pregnancies with early-onset FGR have greater rates of umbilical artery Doppler abnormalities compared with those with late onset.

322
00:45:29,328 --> 00:45:56,378
Similar to pregnancies complicated by preeclampsia, increased maternal serum levels of antiangiogenic factors that include soluble vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 1 (sVEGFR-1) and soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase 1 (sFlt-1) are reported in pregnancies complicated by early-onset growth restriction (Kingdom, 2018; Korzeniewski, 2016) .

323
00:45:56,378 --> 00:46:06,491
Barker Hypothesis Thirty years ago, Barker (1992) hypothesized that adult mortality and morbidity are related to fetal and infant health.

324
00:46:06,491 --> 00:46:09,728
This includes both under- and overgrowth.

325
00:46:09,728 --> 00:46:28,291
In the context of FGR, numerous reports describe a relationship between suboptimal fetal nutrition and a higher risk of subsequent adult hypertension, atherosclerosis, type 2 diabetes, and metabolic derangement (Colella, 2018; Jornayvaz, 2016).

326
00:46:28,291 --> 00:46:42,253
The degree to which low birthweight mediates adult disease is controversial, as weight gain in early life also appears important (Breij, 2014; Kerkhof, 2012; McCloskey, 2016).

327
00:46:42,253 --> 00:46:48,466
Evidence suggests that FGR may affect organ development, particularly that of the heart.

328
00:46:48,466 --> 00:46:57,578
Individuals with low birthweight demonstrate cardiac structural changes and dysfunction that persist through childhood, adolescence, and adulthood .

329
00:46:57,578 --> 00:47:06,716
In one study, 80 infants who were born SGA before 34 weeks’ gestation were compared at 6 months with 80 normally grown infants .

330
00:47:06,716 --> 00:47:13,628
The ventricle in SGA infants was more globular, resulting in systolic and diastolic dysfunction.

331
00:47:13,628 --> 00:47:23,441
In another study, echocardiography in 418 adolescents showed that low birthweight was associated with a thicker left ventricular posterior wall .

332
00:47:23,441 --> 00:47:28,016
However, these findings have unclear long-term significance .

333
00:47:28,016 --> 00:47:34,053
Growth restriction is also associated with postnatal structural and functional renal changes.

334
00:47:34,053 --> 00:47:47,166
Luyckx and Brenner (2015) concluded that both low and high birthweight, maternal obesity, and gestational diabetes adversely affect in-utero development of the kidney and its health into adulthood.

335
00:47:47,166 --> 00:47:57,928
However, other variables that include childhood nutrition, acute kidney injury, excessive childhood weight gain, and obesity also worsen long-term renal function.

336
00:47:57,928 --> 00:47:59,628
#

337
00:47:59,788 --> 00:48:01,438
Prevention.

338
00:48:01,388 --> 00:48:05,163
Ideally, prevention begins before conception.

339
00:48:05,163 --> 00:48:11,963
Maternal medical conditions are treated, and medications are modified to help lower FGR risks.

340
00:48:11,963 --> 00:48:14,663
Smoking cessation is critical.

341
00:48:14,663 --> 00:48:25,163
Other risk factors are tailored to the maternal condition, such as antimalarial prophylaxis for women living in endemic areas and correction of nutritional deficiencies.

342
00:48:25,163 --> 00:48:31,113
Treatment of mild to moderate hypertension does not reduce the incidence of SGA newborns .

343
00:48:31,113 --> 00:48:35,838
Currently, no pharmacologic therapies prevent growth restriction.

344
00:48:35,838 --> 00:48:43,300
Considerable study of low-dose aspirin therapy has not demonstrated consistent benefit in low-risk pregnancies.

345
00:48:43,300 --> 00:48:51,088
Daily aspirin therapy did not reduce the risk for SGA neonates in a randomized trial of 1700 women .

346
00:48:51,088 --> 00:49:09,113
Two large metaanalyses involving more than 20,000 women did find that low-dose aspirin was associated with a significantly lower risk of FGR, whether initiated prior to or after 16 weeks’ gestation (Meher, 2017; Roberge, 2017).

347
00:49:09,113 --> 00:49:16,450
A Cochrane database analysis showed a small reduction in the risk of SGA neonates with maternal aspirin therapy .

348
00:49:16,450 --> 00:49:25,775
However, these studies included women at risk for preeclampsia, and the modest decrease in the risk for growth restriction was a secondary finding.

349
00:49:25,775 --> 00:49:45,263
Because evidence in women without risk factors for preeclampsia is lacking, aspirin therapy for the prevention of FGR is not recommended (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020a, 2021a; Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, 2020) Fetal Macrosomia.

350
00:49:45,263 --> 00:49:47,488
# Definition.

351
00:49:47,488 --> 00:50:00,125
The term macrosomia is used rather imprecisely to describe a fetus whose estimated weight exceeds a threshold—typically 4000 g, 4500 g, or even 5000 g .

352
00:50:00,125 --> 00:50:11,538
Although obstetricians generally agree that neonates weighing <4000 g are not excessively large, a similar consensus has not been reached for the definition of macrosomia.

353
00:50:11,538 --> 00:50:20,025
Large for gestational age (LGA) denotes a fetus or newborn whose weight exceeds the 90th percentile for gestational age.

354
00:50:20,025 --> 00:50:26,238
Similar to SGA, the term LGA does not imply that growth is necessarily abnormal.

355
00:50:26,238 --> 00:50:31,763
Indeed, most such neonates are simply constitutionally larger than their peers.

356
00:50:31,763 --> 00:50:38,925
The 90th percentile for birthweight at 39 weeks’ gestation approximates 3900 g .

357
00:50:38,925 --> 00:50:44,825
Thus, most LGA neonates would not meet any of the common definitions of macrosomia.

358
00:50:44,825 --> 00:51:02,675
In the United States in 2019, 6.4 percent of all newborns weighed 4000 to 4499 g; 0.9 percent weighed 4500 to 4999 g; and 0.1 percent were born weighing ≥5000 g .

359
00:51:02,675 --> 00:51:16,763
Similarly, during a 30-year period at Parkland Hospital, during which more than 350,000 singletons were born, only 1.4 percent of neonates weighed ≥4500 g .

360
00:51:16,763 --> 00:51:25,825
We are of the view that the upper limit of fetal growth, above which growth can be deemed abnormal, is likely to be two standard deviations above the mean.

361
00:51:25,825 --> 00:51:29,450
This represents perhaps 3 percent of births.

362
00:51:29,450 --> 00:51:37,775
At 40 weeks, such a threshold would correspond to approximately 4500 g. Table 47-2.

363
00:51:37,775 --> 00:51:48,300
Birthweight Distribution of 354,509 Liveborn Infants at Parkland Hospital between 1988 and 2012 # Detection.

364
00:51:48,300 --> 00:51:57,188
Because current methods fail to accurately estimate excessive fetal size, macrosomia cannot be definitively diagnosed until delivery .

365
00:51:57,188 --> 00:52:05,775
Inaccuracy in estimates of fetal weight, by either fundal height measurement or sonography, are often attributable to maternal obesity.

366
00:52:05,775 --> 00:52:18,113
In one study of 502 patients with sonographic estimated fetal weights >4000 g within 2 weeks of delivery, the risk of birthweight overestimation was >50 percent .

367
00:52:18,113 --> 00:52:29,050
Of those who underwent cesarean delivery for suspected LGA, almost 30 percent delivered a neonate weighing <4000 g. # Pathophysiology.

368
00:52:29,050 --> 00:52:41,738
Particularly in women with diabetes and elevated cord blood levels of insulin-like growth factor 1, fetal macrosomia is associated with greater neonatal fat mass and morphological heart changes.

369
00:52:41,738 --> 00:52:50,113
Pedersen (1954) first proposed that hyperglycemia leads to fetal hyperinsulinemia and fetal overgrowth.

370
00:52:50,113 --> 00:53:03,725
This has been extended to organ dysmorphia, for example, increased interventricular septal thickness in neonates of mothers with gestational diabetes (Aman, 2011; Garcia-Flores, 2011).

371
00:53:03,725 --> 00:53:07,413
The cardiopulmonary vasculature also is

372
00:53:07,564 --> 00:53:11,239
adversely affected by diabetes in pregnancy.

373
00:53:11,189 --> 00:53:23,514
In 3277 cases of persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn, maternal obesity, diabetes, and both deficient and excessive fetal growth were independent risk factors .

374
00:53:23,514 --> 00:53:32,964
Long-term metabolic consequences of fetal macrosomia in the setting of maternal obesity are discussed in Chapter 51 (p. 907).

375
00:53:32,964 --> 00:53:35,351
# Risk Factors.

376
00:53:35,351 --> 00:53:41,464
Some factors associated with fetal macrosomia are listed in Table 47-3.

377
00:53:41,464 --> 00:53:43,726
Many are interrelated.

378
00:53:43,726 --> 00:53:52,264
Advancing age usually correlates with multiparity and risk for diabetes, and obesity is similarly associated with diabetes.

379
00:53:52,264 --> 00:54:06,264
In one study, the birth prevalence of macrosomia exceeded 24 percent among obese women in China, and macrosomia rates were approximately 2.5-fold higher for prolonged pregnancy and gestational diabetes .

380
00:54:06,264 --> 00:54:09,464
As shown in Table 47-2.

381
00:54:09,464 --> 00:54:15,526
maternal diabetes is strongly associated with neonates weighing >4000 g.

382
00:54:15,526 --> 00:54:32,289
In a prospective study of 682 consecutive pregnancies complicated by diabetes, women with type 1 diabetes were significantly more likely than women with type 2 diabetes to have a neonate weighing above the 90th and 97.7th percentiles .

383
00:54:32,289 --> 00:54:44,001
Higher third-trimester glucose concentration correlates with fetal macrosomia, and hemoglobin A1c and fasting glucose values are independent predictors of macrosomia risk .

384
00:54:44,001 --> 00:54:51,264
Notably, maternal diabetes is associated with only a small percentage of the total number of LGA newborns.

385
00:54:51,264 --> 00:54:53,789
TABLE 47-3.

386
00:54:53,789 --> 00:54:58,076
Risk Factors for Fetal Macrosomia # Management.

387
00:54:58,076 --> 00:55:04,201
Several interventions have been proposed to interdict suspected or “impending” fetal overgrowth.

388
00:55:04,201 --> 00:55:12,589
Exercise in pregnancy is beneficial to the mother, does not increase the risk for growth impairment, and decreases macrosomia risk.

389
00:55:12,589 --> 00:55:30,026
One metaanalysis of 28 studies involving 5322 women concluded that exercise reduces the risk of an LGA newborn or birthweight >4000 g without raising the risk of an SGA neonate or birthweight <2500 g .

390
00:55:30,026 --> 00:55:41,239
Similarly, others have concluded that aerobic exercise increases the likelihood of a normal weight neonate (Di Mascio, 2016; Perales, 2016).

391
00:55:41,239 --> 00:55:53,151
For women with diabetes, insulin therapy and glycemic control may lower the prevalence of neonatal macrosomia but have not consistently translated into reduced cesarean delivery rates.

392
00:55:53,151 --> 00:56:08,951
Fetal macrosomia, irrespective of the diagnosis of diabetes mellitus, is strongly associated with maternal obesity and excessive gestational weight gain (Durie, 2011; Durst, 2016; Harper, 2015).

393
00:56:08,951 --> 00:56:17,076
Currently recommended weight gains for pregnancy according to maternal BMI are described in Chapter 10 (p. 183).

394
00:56:17,076 --> 00:56:24,939
Prophylactic” Labor Induction Some clinicians have induced labor when fetal macrosomia was suspected in nondiabetic women.

395
00:56:24,939 --> 00:56:34,464
The rationale for this approach was to obviate further fetal growth and, in theory, reduce the risk of delivery complications or cesarean delivery.

396
00:56:34,464 --> 00:56:48,201
In a systematic review of 11 studies of expectant management compared with labor induction for suspected macrosomia, labor induction significantly increased cesarean delivery rates without improving perinatal outcomes .

397
00:56:48,201 --> 00:57:08,514
In contrast, Magro-Malosso and colleagues (2017) performed a metaanalysis of four randomized trials involving 1190 women and concluded that labor induction at ≥38 weeks’ gestation for suspected macrosomia significantly reduces the frequency of fetal overgrowth and fractures.

398
00:57:08,514 --> 00:57:18,264
In one of these studies, 822 women with suspected LGA fetuses were randomly assigned either to early-term delivery or to expectant management .

399
00:57:18,264 --> 00:57:25,139
There was a higher rate of vaginal delivery that was marginally significant and a lower composite measure of morbidity.

400
00:57:25,139 --> 00:57:32,101
The authors cautioned that any benefits should be balanced with the risks of early-term labor induction and delivery.

401
00:57:32,101 --> 00:57:43,851
Namely, a review of early-term births indicates that elective delivery before 39 weeks’ gestation does not improve maternal outcomes and is associated with worse neonatal outcomes .

402
00:57:43,851 --> 00:57:58,089
We agree with the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a, 2020b) that current evidence does not support a policy for early labor induction or delivery before 39 weeks’ gestation.

403
00:57:58,089 --> 00:57:59,689
Elective

404
00:57:59,860 --> 00:58:11,497
Cesarean Delivery With the delivery of macrosomic neonates, shoulder dystocia and its attendant risks described in Chapter 27 (p. 501) are major concerns.

405
00:58:11,447 --> 00:58:16,535
That said, 9 percent of these injuries still follow cesarean delivery .

406
00:58:16,535 --> 00:58:26,785
Therefore, planned cesarean delivery on the basis of suspected macrosomia to prevent brachial plexopathy is an unreasonable strategy in the general population .

407
00:58:26,785 --> 00:58:38,760
Ecker and coworkers (1997) analyzed 80 cases of brachial plexus injury in 77,616 consecutive newborns at Brigham and Women’s Hospital.

408
00:58:38,760 --> 00:58:49,835
They concluded that an excessive number of otherwise unnecessary cesarean deliveries would be needed to prevent a single brachial plexus injury in neonates born to women without diabetes.

409
00:58:49,835 --> 00:58:58,747
Others echoed these sentiments in their analysis of nondiabetic mothers (Rouse, 1996; Van der Looven, 2020).

410
00:58:58,747 --> 00:59:22,935
Conversely, planned cesarean delivery may be a reasonable strategy for diabetic women with an estimated fetal weight >4250 or >4500 g. Conway and Langer (1998) described a protocol of routine cesarean delivery for sonographic estimates of ≥4250 g in diabetic women.

411
00:59:22,935 --> 00:59:29,710
This management significantly lowered the shoulder dystocia rate from 2.4 to 1.1 percent.

412
00:59:29,710 --> 00:59:43,010
In summary, we agree with the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists that elective delivery for the fetus that is suspected to be overgrown is inadvisable, particularly before 39 weeks’ gestation.

413
00:59:43,010 --> 01:00:03,060
Last, we also conclude that elective cesarean delivery is not indicated when estimated fetal weight is <5000 g among women without diabetes and <4500 g among women with diabetes (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019a, 2020b).

414
01:00:03,060 --> 01:00:05,272
# Outcomes.

415
01:00:05,272 --> 01:00:09,922
The adverse consequences of excessive fetal growth are considerable.

416
01:00:09,922 --> 01:00:16,672
Neonates with a birthweight of at least 4000 g have cesarean delivery rates exceeding 50 percent.

417
01:00:16,672 --> 01:00:30,197
This is particularly true with maternal obesity or diabetes or with birthweights >5000 g (Cordero, 2015; Crosby, 2017; Hehir, 2015).

418
01:00:30,197 --> 01:00:36,322
Neonatal morbidity is higher in LGA neonates compared with those with lower birthweights.

419
01:00:36,322 --> 01:00:48,285
Macrosomic newborns have higher rates of shoulder dystocia, obstetrical brachial plexus injuries, and birth fractures (Beta, 2019; Chauhan, 2017).

420
01:00:48,285 --> 01:00:56,922
Rates of shoulder dystocia vary greatly and can reach nearly 30 percent for macrosomic neonates when maternal diabetes is comorbid .

421
01:00:56,922 --> 01:01:12,035
In general obstetrical populations that include diabetic mothers, dystocia rates are at least 5 percent for neonates with birthweights ≥5000 g (Crosby, 2017; Hehir, 2015).

422
01:01:12,035 --> 01:01:18,460
The risk for stillbirth is greater with macrosomia, and this risk rises with increasing birth weight .

423
01:01:18,460 --> 01:01:29,172
Rates of postpartum hemorrhage, perineal laceration, and maternal infection, which are related complications, also are higher in mothers delivering overgrown newborns.

424
01:01:29,172 --> 01:01:38,260
Table 47-4 shows maternal and neonatal outcomes by birthweight for neonates >4000 g delivered at Parkland Hospital.

425
01:01:38,260 --> 01:01:40,872
